title: 5. Why one round of reading, Tay first
pid: 4b79a948-199d-430a-8047-e2ce67e67551
body: |2-
  Tay goes first, because if the request is not worth doing Libby's
  reading is wasted. Each brief says: report only what would change
  what gets built, or whether it can be followed; skip style. A reader
  told to find problems always finds some, and fixing every one bloats
  the work. Each writes a short note in its own graph, a headline per
  change, most important first, as Tay did on 2026-11-12 in
  [[id:d7252567-aa4b-40b4-abf5-7dd7053a8d4e][Tay's reading of Claude's design for consuming another's changes]],
  which turned eight questions for Jeff into one, and as Libby did in
  [[id:0e49947f-c5ec-488a-a6b4-65b5bc5594de][Libby's reading of Vic's plan]]. If Tay says not to do it, the plan
  becomes 'abandoned'; if Tay cuts it to a different request, it goes
  back to its author.
  First use: Vic, the author, folded Tay's, Libby's and Drew's findings
  into the plan at the start of the mission that built part 2; Drew,
  who built part 1 before that, worked from the plan and the readings,
  and answered the questions put to him in his report.
  Rests on: read (Claude Code's best practices: a fresh reviewer, and
  "skip style"; Nygard: a record is a conversation with a future
  developer). Ours: one round, the order, the builder folding in.
